around the world many governments have  been propping up their economies with  stimulus packages  to tackle the devastating effects of the  covid19 pandemic  america alone has spent trillions of  dollars it could be about to spend  trillions more  negotiations will continue today on a  second round of stimulus money and  unemployment benefits from the federal  government  the long-term impact of these  unprecedented fiscal measures  remains to be seen you submitted your  questions about america's stimulus  to john fassman our washington  correspondent  and callum williams as senior economics  writer  here are their answers where does the  stimulus money come from  i mean fundamentally it comes from the  us government  going out into the markets and and  borrowing money so at the moment the u.s  government is taking in  much less in uh taxes than it wishes to  spend and  is spending and so basically it has to  go to investors and say  we would like to borrow some money off  you and we will pay you for that  privilege and fortunately the conditions  are such that  the us government is able to borrow  really really vast amounts  trillions of dollars and uh that's not  going to cost them  all all that much given um given how  given that interest rates are fairly low  calum given that the american economy is  in such a state why is it still so easy  for the government to borrow  i think broadly speaking the answer is  to do with the fact that there's  really high demand at the moment and has  been for a while for kind of  safe assets and really the safest asset  in the whole world  is us government debt will the funds  actually reach the people who need it  most  you'll remember when the first round of  federal unemployment  stimulus came out it took a while for  that money to reach people because state  unemployment systems  are so creaky that they had to  effectively redesign them  to take in this new stream of funds the  worry about  trump's executive order on ui now is  that with 300  coming from the federal government and  100 coming from the states it's another  new  pair of income streams which means the  states will need to configure their  systems  to get that money to people which means  it will take some time to actually get  the money to the people who need it most  but john i guess also it's the case  right that there are large  sort of chunks of people in the u.s who  maybe were not even  eligible for say the stimulus checks  which were paid out in april  and maybe even still haven't got their  unemployment benefits per cap perhaps  because they have an irregular  immigration status and  all that kind of thing yeah i think  that's true there is a risk that some  people who need the money may not be  able to get it at all and remember that  the amount of money  that trump made available through his  executive order is just 44 billion so  that will last maybe six weeks  so whether people will get their money  within six weeks is an open question  and then after that the money goes away  so i think we'll probably see another  sort of  pinch later this fall how does the  american economic stimulus compare to  other countries globally  i mean there are a few other countries  like britain japan  singapore which get quite close but  america  is pretty pretty certainly the most  generous of the whole world  and that's i think particularly  extraordinary given how  stingy the american welfare state  generally is and how deep the reluctance  is to give people money when they're out  of work  in the u.s for complicated historical  reasons  every time there's a recession there  have to be these  these very prolonged negotiations that  congress about exactly how the us  government is going to make unemployment  insurance more generous so  the u.s kind of has to be more generous  when it comes to a recession  and this is part of the debate that's in  congress right now right senators like  michael bennett want the  ui benefits to have stabilizers meaning  that as until  unemployment one proposal is until the  unemployment rate is back at  pre-pandemic levels  some stimulus will keep going out it  will gradually be tapered down  so there's a move among some democrats  in particular in congress to push  the next stimulus measure whenever that  is to include these stabilizers so it  does go out automatically  has the increased unemployment benefit  disincentivized people from going back  to work  no it hasn't there is not much work for  people to go back to  so people are getting this money not as  an alternative to actually finding work  but because so many businesses have  closed because so many businesses are  hurting  because demand is down so much this  money is going out  to make sure the entire economy doesn't  seize up and people still have money  to pay for food rent and things like  that i mean i think  i think it is true that like there are  many instances in which  generous unemployment insurance but  payments can make it less likely that  people look for work  it's not like a myth that that happens  but at the moment as john says  there's just absolutely no evidence that  it's discouraging people from working  there is this sort of doctrinal  reluctance especially among the american  right to giving people money  to giving people generous amounts of  money who are out of work because it  does in fact it can  disincentivize work but in this case  that's just that's just not true there's  no work  how will america's economic stimulus  impact inflation  so you hear this argument a lot um on  you know from certain people that  because the us is uh  is is pumping so much money into the  economy and the supply of money is  rising quickly  the inflation is definitely going to be  around the corner um  my sense is that that is not at the  moment doesn't look very likely at all  and the  the reason for that basically is that  what you're seeing at the moment is a  lot of very cautious behavior  both among firms and among households so  for instance with households  the share of income that people are  saving has gone way way up since the  pandemic began  and you're seeing the same thing with  businesses where businesses aren't  investing really  as much anywhere near as much as they  were they're conserving their money  instead so  although money's being paid out it's not  so clear that there's a lot of  there's a lot of extra money kind of  chasing goods and services which  ultimately is what  is what causes inflation to go wages  just aren't really going to be going up  that much and fundamentally to have  inflation  persistent high inflation you need to  have wages that are also going up  otherwise inflation basically sort of  runs out of steam  so at the moment i just don't see that  you know stimulus having a particularly  large impact on  inflation should americans be concerned  that the stimulus will put the  government  in debt my  my sense is um that it's it's  it's okay and legitimate to worry about  debt sustainability um there is  obviously a point  at which a country is overly indebted i  don't think the us is at that stage yet  um not least because interest rates are  very low so the share of  you know federal money that's going on  um interest repayments is quite low by  historical standards but also  i think you have to make a judgment call  as to whether  having a bit of extra or quite a lot of  extra debt is  worse than a recession  which without the government help would  have been really quite  staggeringly bad so um  i think that's the letter of two evils  really but that doesn't mean that debt  it  has no political salience i think  especially if you see  joe biden win the white house i think  there's going to be a tremendous concern  about national debt that comes from  republicans who have heretofore been  quiet both regarding the effects of  donald trump's tax cut  and the amount of money going out the  door in the stimulus politically i  expect concern  over the debt is going to rise quite  steeply if joe biden wins so john does  that mean that if we  if if biden wins that basically  unless the democrats take both houses of  congress you could basically rule out  any further fiscal stimulus  i would think so i think unless  democrats take both houses of congress  then i think you'll see a tremendous  push for austerity does that mean  that purely from an economic perspective  you you want trump to win  that is a fantastic question because if  trump wins i think never democrats and  republicans alike  democrats want to get money out the door  generally republicans will be more  willing with trump in the white house  you know if you have democratic control  of both houses of congress  and the white house i think you'll see  stimulus continue  but debt will be the issue on which  republicans go charging into the  midterms  can the positive effects of the stimulus  on the u.s economy be shown  if you look at what's happened to  household income in the u.s since the  crisis began  normally in a recession it goes down  kind of obviously because people lose  jobs and all that sort of stuff  whereas so far the this year it has gone  up which is  you know highly highly unusual so in  that very sort of simplistic sense it  has  had a positive impact on the economy  it's quite hard to  point to any obvious negative effects  that it's had  so on balance i think it's a pretty  compelling case it's done a lot of good  i think one other positive effect of  stimulus rate can be seen in  in the stabilization of poverty rates  that even as the economy contracted  and and and people lost their jobs the  poverty rate did not  measurably increase that suggests to me  that the stimulus kept people from  falling into poverty who otherwise would  have  is america becoming more socialist by  handing out the stimulus  [Music]  no i mean the workers have not  controlled the means of production  still a country of private businesses  most americans are capitalist to their  bones there's that  great john steinbeck quotes that every  poor person in america is a temporarily  embarrassed millionaire  i think that still holds true in this  case though there are a whole lot of  people who went out of work for no fault  of their own  and who need a lot of help and the  economy in general benefits from them  getting that help  so is that a socialist idea if it's a  socialist idea i guess i guess it is but  i don't think america is in  any danger of becoming a socialist  country is it possible that there is  because um the government was able to  come together  and basically spend a lot of money very  quickly  and also spending money on things that i  think in normal times would have seemed  completely outrageous  like giving people more in unemployment  they got from their job  is it possible that in the future it  sort of  opens the door towards more radical  ideas being entertained i mean  there are clearly radical ideas being  entertained that would not have been  entertained five years ago things like a  universal basic income medicare for all  those have not come about because of the  pandemic  they came about because of the  conditions that sort of prevented a  strong national response to the pandemic  so i think there is a groundswell  for those sorts of broad get money out  the door  um ideas that would have been considered  far too left  to even contemplate five or ten years  ago there is support for those  but that's distinct i think from the  response to the pandemic i think the  response to the pandemic  is is is so suey generous that it's it's  it's hard to see it laying the  groundwork  for future sort of similarly radical  ideas just because the space for that  the conditions for that would have to be  met i think the the sort of shift left  word  occurred for reasons unrelated to the  pandemic and will keep occurring for  releases unrelated to the pandemic  i'm jon fasstman the economist  washington correspondent you can stay up  to date with the issues surrounding the  pandemic and politics in america  by subscribing to our checks and balance  newsletter just click on the link  opposite  thank you for watching

